# Archer Caddyfile - two genuinely different access patterns, handled differently
#
# There is only ONE archer.py process and ONE port (verified against the
# current source: exactly one Flask() instance and one .run() call, at the
# bottom of archer.py). Tiers 1-4 are NOT separate servers on separate
# ports - they're all routes on the same app, and get_request_tier()
# resolves which dashboard a visitor sees from their MAC/JWT/PIN.
#
# PRIVATE (family): Tailscale-only. Bound to the box's own Tailscale IP,
# not a domain name - confirmed working in production this way. A bare
# `:8080 { bind <ip> ... }` site address does NOT trigger Caddy's automatic
# HTTPS (a bare port has no "host" for Caddy to provision a certificate
# for); giving it an actual hostname or IP AS THE SITE ADDRESS instead of
# via `bind` does trigger it and breaks plain HTTP - confirmed live, the
# hard way, in this exact deployment. Don't "simplify" this back to
# `<ip>:8080 { reverse_proxy ... }` - it looks equivalent and isn't.
#
# PUBLIC (fan page): genuinely internet-facing, on a different port so it
# never collides with the private block above. Path-restricted at THIS
# layer to an explicit allowlist, deliberately NOT "reverse_proxy the
# whole app and let archer.py's tier system be the only gate" (that was
# this file's original design, matching the existing HuggingFace Space
# deployment's lack of network-level gating - see git history / README
# "Real open questions" for that reasoning). Diverges from it on purpose:
# once Caddy reverse-proxies a request, it arrives at Flask as 127.0.0.1
# regardless of the real client's address, which silently neuters any
# route that treats loopback as trusted (e.g. panic mode's
# _is_tailscale_or_loopback() network gate - Tier-1 JWT auth still blocks
# an unauthenticated caller, but the IP-level backstop stops meaning
# anything for traffic that arrived through a whole-app-proxied public
# block). An explicit path allowlist means a route that was never meant to
# be internet-facing gets a 404 from Caddy before it ever reaches
# archer.py, independent of whatever archer.py's own auth does or doesn't
# catch.
#
# Replace every placeholder below (marked with <>) with real values.

# --- PRIVATE: family access (phone, watch, Roku) ---
:8080 {
    bind <your-tailscale-ip>

    # 7860 confirmed from archer.py: `port = int(os.environ.get('PORT', 7860))`,
    # and matches the Dockerfile's `PORT=7860 python3 archer.py`. Override with
    # self-host/archer.service's PORT env var if you ever change it — keep
    # this port number in sync with that file if you do.
    # The Pi (tag:pi) may only make its own four calls. A Tailscale ACL can
    # only filter by port, so once the Pi is allowed to reach :8080 at all it
    # could otherwise reach this whole block (login, display_data, ...) — this
    # closes that at the path level, for that one source IP only. Exact paths,
    # not a `/terminal/pi_*` prefix: /terminal/pi_status is a Tier-1 read the
    # Pi never calls. Every other source is unaffected. Set the placeholder to
    # the Pi's own Tailscale IP (`tailscale ip -4` on the Pi); with no Pi in
    # the deployment, delete the @pi_denied matcher and its respond line.
    # `route` is required for the same ordering reason as the public block.
    @pi_denied {
        remote_ip <your-pi-tailscale-ip>
        not path /csrf_token /terminal/pi_register /terminal/pi_disconnect /terminal/pi_config_check
    }
    route {
        respond @pi_denied 403
        reverse_proxy 127.0.0.1:7860
    }
}

# --- PUBLIC: fan page, genuinely internet-facing ---
# Requires ports 80 actually forwarded/reachable on this box - separate
# from Tailscale entirely, no domain name or DNS record needed for plain
# HTTP on a bare port (see the note above on why NOT to give this block an
# actual hostname/IP as its site address).
#
# The allowlist below is every route confirmed, by reading the actual
# code, to be both (a) designed to be public - blueprints/fans.py's own
# header comment: "All routes here are public (no tier auth required)" -
# and (b) free of the fields _filter_display_data_for_tier() exists to
# protect (destination/gps_lat/gps_lon/gps_name/surveillance/cameras/
# valet_events/parking_active/parking_loc). /display_data is included
# because the fan page's own embedded JS depends on it for the live vitals
# ticker (archer_fan.html fetches it directly) and because an anonymous
# caller resolves to tier 4 there, which the filter strips those same
# fields for - confirmed via TestDisplayDataSensitiveFieldFiltering, not
# assumed. Nothing else archer.py serves is in this list; add a route here
# only after the same two checks, not by widening the path matcher.
:80 {
    @public path /fans /fan /register /csrf_token /register_mac /fans/ask /fans/react /fans/stats /display_data
    # `route` is required here, not optional style — Caddy's Caddyfile
    # directives run in a fixed built-in order, NOT the order written in
    # this file, and `respond` runs before `reverse_proxy` in that order.
    # Without `route`, respond 404 fires unconditionally for every request
    # regardless of the matcher, before reverse_proxy ever gets a chance -
    # confirmed live with a throwaway instance on scratch ports, the exact
    # failure mode this comment exists to prevent someone from reintroducing.
    route {
        reverse_proxy @public 127.0.0.1:7860
        respond 404
    }
}
